WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.204 --> 00:00:02.370
DUSTIN INGRAM: I'm Dustin
Ingram, developer advocate

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.350
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.750
I'm really excited to announce
the general availability

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:11.106
of Python 3 Runtime on the App
Engine standard environment.

00:00:11.106 --> 00:00:12.980
Python is one of the
most popular programming

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.864
languages and growing.

00:00:14.864 --> 00:00:17.030
It's well suited for a
number of use cases including

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:20.870
web applications, machine
learning, and big data.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.880
App Engine has
supported Python Runtime

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.060
from the very beginning,
more than 10 years now.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.345
You may have tried Python
on App Engine in the past

00:00:28.345 --> 00:00:30.470
and noticed that it was
very opinionated about what

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.720
you could and couldn't do.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.960
This new Python Runtime
is very different.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.860
Python 3.7 on App Engine is
our second generation Runtime.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.050
That means it's based
on open source Python

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.450
from the Python
Software Foundation.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.610
It supports Google
Cloud client libraries

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:51.530
for services like Cloud storage,
vision, translate, et cetera.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.660
You can install
libraries, frameworks,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.750
and other dependencies
using requirements.txt,

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.050
and best of all, Python 3.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.820
I like to deploy web
apps to App Engine

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.650
because my app
scales automatically

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:07.220
up or down, even down to
zero, to meet incoming traffic

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:08.750
spikes.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.970
I only need to think
about my Python code,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.270
not the infrastructure or
wiring that runs my app.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:16.340
And I only pay for
what my app uses,

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:21.070
and I can use App Engine's free
tier to easily try new things.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.810
In this video, I'll
show you how to use

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.690
Flask, a popular and
versatile web framework,

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:29.050
to quickly write, deploy,
and test a Python 3 web

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.180
app on App Engine.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.940
Once we've got the app deployed,
we'll iterate on it a bit

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.550
to take advantage of some
of those great new features.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:39.230
There are three files
that will make up our app.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:43.330
app.yaml, main.py,
and requirements.txt.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.640
Let's start with app.yaml.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.290
This file lets you
configure your app

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:49.330
in a number of different ways.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:52.210
All we need to do in this
file is tell App Engine

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:55.870
that we want to use the
new Python 3.7 Runtime.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.540
We do this by
adding these lines.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.970
Next, moving down
our list of files,

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:05.730
let's define our app's behavior
by creating our main.py file.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:09.350
This is where we can define the
core logic of our application.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.180
We'll call it main.py
because that's

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.306
what App Engine uses
by default. But you

00:02:13.306 --> 00:02:15.680
can call it whatever you want
as long as you configure it

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.480
appropriately.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.670
We'll use Flask as
our web framework,

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:22.960
but you can use any
framework you'd like as well.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.590
By default, App Engine
looks for a variable called

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.290
app inside your main.py file.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:31.710
As you might guess, this
should correspond to your app.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.410
We'll define an end point at
the root URL that just says

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.060
hello, world, and that's it.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.400
Finally, the new Python
runtime understands

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.900
the same requirements
format that

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.990
is used by PIP and other
package installers and Runtimes

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.400
across the entire
Python ecosystem,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.730
the requirements.txt file.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.310
Because we've chosen Flask
as our web framework,

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.040
we'll specify it
as a dependency.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.340
In a file called
requirements.txt,

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.480
we'll add Flask
and pin its version

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.280
to be exactly version 1.0.2, the
latest version that's currently

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.770
available.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.340
Pinning the version
of a dependency

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:08.470
ensures that the next
time you deploy your app,

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:10.990
you won't install a newer
and possibly incompatible

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.570
version of your dependency,
which could lead to breakage.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:16.560
So now we've defined
these three files--

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.170
app.yaml, main.py,
and requirements.txt.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.470
Our app is finished.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:23.350
Let's deploy it.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.710
Deploying is easy with the
gcloud command line tool.

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:29.140
Once you've installed it
and authenticated yourself,

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.990
all you need to do is
type gcloud app deploy

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:34.480
to deploy your
app to App Engine.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.090
Now that it's done, we
can type gcloud app browse

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.830
to view the app.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.120
Looks great.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.040
Now let's see what
else we can do.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:45.070
Web apps are usually most useful
when they can accept input as

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:46.810
well as produce output.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.350
What if we just want to say
hello to a specific person?

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.540
Let's see how we can
do this with Flask.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.610
By importing the
request object, we

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:58.120
can inspect the query string
parameters from the URL.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.790
We'll look for one called name.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:04.920
Let's deploy that real quick
with gcloud app deploy.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.560
If we don't pass any
parameters, it stays the same

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.620
and says hi to the whole world.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:12.660
But if we add a name=Python3
parameter to the end

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:16.320
of the URL, we've now passed
that value into our app,

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.029
and it's rendered
as you would expect.

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:21.300
Since this is Python
3, we can make our code

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.500
even better by using f strings
instead of string concatenation

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.100
or by sticking a few emojis
right into our source code.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.230
Putting the emoji in
line like that is great,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:32.730
but what if we
wanted the user to be

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.130
able to include an emoji
of their own choice

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.720
by specifying it as text?

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.320
We could create a massive
mapping from emoji names

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.900
to their actual values
and find and replace them

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.730
in our name parameter.

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:48.030
Instead, let's use this
third party emoji package,

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.760
which is open source and
available on the Python Package

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.200
Index.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.120
This package provides
an emojize function,

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.110
which will let us
turn text into emojis.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.120
All we need to do is
add the dependency

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.700
to our requirements.txt
file like so.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:06.070
Remember, always pin
your dependencies.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:09.210
Now, in main.py, we can
import the emojize function

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.700
from the emoji library,
and then use it

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.790
to emojize the entire greeting.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:19.069
Once again, we'll deploy
it with gcloud app deploy.

00:05:19.069 --> 00:05:20.610
Now, let's add the
string colon snake

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.520
colon to our name
parameter and see what

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.010
it looks like in our browser.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.030
Looks great.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:29.610
So much more customizable.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.830
So that's all there is
to making a Python 3

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.440
app that handles user
input and uses third party

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.630
libraries from PyPI.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.400
Stay tuned for the
next episode, where

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.180
I'll show you how to deploy
a wag tail app to App Engine.

